Introduction
In an earlier article I built a per-setup trade ledger. It put a Wilson interval around the win rate of each entry type in an EA. In the review the moderator asked what assumptions that interval rests on. The interval treats the trades as Bernoulli trials: each trade wins with the same probability, and the result of one trade tells you nothing about the result of the next. I understood both conditions. I had never checked either of them on my own trades.
The second condition also sits under a rule I see in many EAs and trading plans. Stop for the day after 3 losses in a row, or cut the lot after a loss. A pause like this can serve several purposes, such as a daily loss limit or a cooling-off period for the trader. This article looks at one purpose only, the idea that the trade after a losing streak is more likely to lose. As a filter of that kind, the rule only helps if losses come in clusters. If they don't, the pause skips trades with the same chance of winning as any other trade.
Two articles on this site already point at the gap. Kayode Michael Oyetunde's article on hypothesis testing [1] warns that win and loss streaks make p-values look better than they are. The Monte Carlo stress test article [2] says it does not model serial correlation between trades. Neither of them tests for it.
This article implements the test as a small set of include files. They read the closed trades of an account or a tester run and convert them into a win/loss sequence in the order the trades closed. The Wald-Wolfowitz runs test [3] is applied to that sequence. Because traders think in streaks more than in runs, the module adds three streak checks: the longest losing streak, the loss rate of every trade after 3 or more losses, and the loss rate of the first trade after exactly 3 losses. Each check is compared with 10,000 shuffled orders of the same trades. A fourth check tests the other condition, a win rate that stays the same over time.
I run the module on two of my own EAs. The first is the SMC scalper from the ledger article on USDCAD, with five M1 runs in 2026, one M1 run in 2025, and one M15 run. The second is a gold version of the same idea, tested on 2025 and 2026 data. None of the checks detected dependence at the 5% level. The closest result, one setup losing more often right after 3 losses, did not repeat on the 2025 data. The article also shows how large an effect each check could have missed, and for some samples that effect is large.
What a run is
A run is a block of identical outcomes in a row. A sequence of eight trades that ended W W L L L W L W forms five runs: WW, LLL, W, L, and W.
If losses tend to follow losses and wins follow wins, the blocks get longer and there are fewer of them. If the outcomes flip more often than chance would give, the blocks get shorter and there are more of them. The test counts the runs and asks how unusual that count is for this many wins and losses, if every order of them were equally likely.
With n1 wins, n2 losses, and n = n1 + n2 trades, the expected number of runs under independence is 2 × n1 × n2 / n + 1. The variance is 2 × n1 × n2 × (2 × n1 × n2 − n) / (n² × (n − 1)). Fewer runs than expected points to clustering. More runs than expected points to alternation. I use a two-sided test, so either direction counts as evidence against independence.
Only the order of wins and losses matters. The test doesn't look at how much a trade won or lost, so a run of tiny losses and a run of large ones count the same. It also compares each trade only with its neighbor. Keep both limits in mind when you read the results.
How the pieces fit together
The module consists of four include files, two scripts, and a Python script. Fig. 1 shows how data moves through them.
Data flow of the runs test module
From the deal history to the journal and to the Python check
Fig. 1. Data flow from the deal history to the printed report and the Python cross-check
CTradeSequence reads the deals and produces one win or loss per closed trade. CRunsTest does the runs test. CStreakStats does the streak checks and the shuffling. BlockWinRates() splits the trades into five consecutive blocks and tests whether their win rates differ. RunsTestPrintReport() calls all of them and prints one block of lines. It can be called from the RunsTestReport.mq5 script on a live account, or from the OnDeinit() of any EA at the end of a test. RunsTestExportDeals() writes the raw deals to a CSV file, and runs_crosscheck.py rebuilds the whole report from that file without any MQL5 code.
Turning deal history into wins and losses: TradeSequence.mqh
One position, one outcome
MetaTrader keeps deals, not trades. A position opened once and closed in two parts leaves three deals: the entry and two exits. If every exit deal counted as a trade, one partial close would add an extra outcome and change the run count for no real reason. The moderator raised this exact problem with the history rebuild in my ledger class, so the grouping by DEAL_POSITION_ID [5] was there from the start.
The second detail is commission. On many accounts part of the commission is charged on the entry deal. A trade whose exit shows +0.50, with 0.70 charged on entry, lost 0.20. If you read only the exit deals it looks like a win. AddDeal() therefore adds the net of every deal to its trade, the entry included. It also keeps the volume that went in and the volume that came out. The volume is how Finalize() later decides whether a trade was closed.
