The magic number is taken from the entry deal. The EA sets the magic on the order it sends, and I didn't want to depend on what the exit deal carries when a stop-loss or take-profit closes the position. The test script feeds exits with magic 0 on purpose, to make sure the entry value is the one that counts.
On a netting account a reversal is a single DEAL_ENTRY_INOUT deal, and the position keeps its identifier. AddDeal() splits that deal in two. The part of its volume that closes the old trade takes the profit and its share of the costs, and the rest opens a new trade with the same position ID. Splitting the costs by volume is my own assumption. A unit test covers the split, but none of my test runs used a netting account.
After a reversal, two records share one position ID, so the lookup decides where the later deals go. Find() scans the records from the end and returns the latest one with that ID. Append() creates the second record.
