The normal-approximation p-values use a 0.5 continuity correction, applied toward the mean. The Z() function returns the plain score without the correction, because that is the number people usually compare with 1.96.
Exact p-values, and a library function that returned NaN
The exact distribution of the run count is known. For an even count r = 2k the probability is 2 × C(n1 − 1, k − 1) × C(n2 − 1, k − 1) / C(n, n1). For an odd count r = 2k + 1 it is [C(n1 − 1, k) × C(n2 − 1, k − 1) + C(n1 − 1, k − 1) × C(n2 − 1, k)] / C(n, n1). The binomial coefficients get astronomically large for a few hundred trades, so everything is done in logarithms.
The standard library already has MathBinomialCoefficientLog() in Math\Stat\Math.mqh [6], so I used it. The first compile stopped with error 182, "ambiguous call to overloaded function". When I opened the library I found two versions of the function, one taking int arguments and one taking double. My call mixed a double and an int, so the compiler couldn't choose. That error was easy to fix. The int version builds the coefficient by multiplying in a long, and for large arguments the long overflows. The result is the logarithm of a negative number. In my test script C(62,31) through the int version prints "-nan(ind)", while the double version gives 40.681735.
All my other calls in that file passed int arguments. Once the first error was fixed, they would have compiled without a word and gone straight to the int version. The values 62 and 31 correspond to roughly 63 wins and 63 losses, which almost any EA history passes. So the test would have returned NaN on real data while working on every small example I tried by hand. Now every call goes through a helper that only accepts doubles:
