For large samples the exact version adds little. With 210 wins, 190 losses, and 185 runs, the exact lower tail is 0.066038 and the normal one 0.066078. With seven wins, five losses, and three runs the exact value is 0.0152 and the normal 0.0187, about 23% higher. People usually run a test like this on small samples, when they're worried about a new EA. So I compute the exact value everywhere and print the normal one next to it as a check.
Four verdicts
The moderator's review of my ledger article made one point I took into this design. "Not enough data" and "evidence against" are different answers and should not share a label. So the verdict has four states:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Verdict of the runs test                                         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_RUNS_VERDICT
  {
   RUNS_TOO_FEW,
// not enough wins or losses to judge
RUNS_NOT_DETECTED,
// run count is inside the normal range
RUNS_CLUSTERED,
// fewer runs than chance: streaks or drift
RUNS_ALTERNATING
// more runs than chance: flip-flopping
};
The wording of two of them is deliberate. A run count inside the normal range is reported as "no dependence detected by the runs test", not as "independent", because the test can only fail to find a pattern. A run count that is too low is reported as "clustering or drift", because a win rate that changes over time also produces fewer runs. The section on drift below shows how to tell the two apart.
The obvious way to decide "too few" is a fixed minimum number of trades. I kept one (10 wins and 10 losses by default), but it's a judgment call. The other rule is not. For some counts the test can't reject independence whatever the order is. With 4 wins and 4 losses, even the most extreme orders, LLLLWWWW or LWLWLWLW, give a two-sided p of 0.0571. Calling that result "not detected" would be misleading, because no detection was possible. MinAchievableP() computes the smallest p the counts allow, and if that is above alpha the verdict is "too few":
