The two-sided p-value is twice the smaller tail, capped at 1. Twice the smaller tail is one of several conventions for a discrete distribution that isn't symmetric. I picked it because it's the easiest one to check by hand.
Streaks a trader would recognize: StreakStats.mqh
The longest losing streak
A runs test answers a question most traders never ask. The question they do ask is closer to "I just lost 21 in a row. Is something broken?" CStreakStats answers that by shuffling. CStreakStats takes the same wins and losses and puts them in random order, 10,000 times by default. Then it counts how often a losing streak at least as long appears. If it happens often, the streak is what chance produces with that many losses.
The module uses a Fisher-Yates shuffle. MathRand() returns only 0 to 32767 [6], which can't pick a position in a history longer than that, so RandomIndex() combines two calls into 30 bits. Taking that number modulo the range would favor low indexes slightly, so values above the largest multiple of the range are rejected and drawn again. For 1,405 trades the bias this removes is about one part in a million, far below anything the report could show. The fix takes three lines. MathSrand() is set from an input seed so the same report can be reproduced exactly.
