My first thought was to compare these rates with the overall loss rate. Before writing the comparison I tried the shuffle on a toy sequence of 20 losses followed by 20 wins. In shuffled versions of it, with no dependence at all, I expected the loss rate after 3 or more losses to be a little under 50%. Three of the losses are already used up. The shuffled rate came out at 39.5% (test case 22 below).
This is the bias Miller and Sanjurjo described in their work on the hot hand [4]. In a finite sequence, the share of successes right after a streak of successes is biased downwards, and in short sequences the bias is large. Part of it is the used-up losses I expected. The rest comes from averaging a proportion over sequences in which only one or two trades follow a streak. For a trader it means that "68% of my trades after 3 losses were losses, and my loss rate is 65%" proves nothing on its own. On a short history the fair comparison is with the shuffled rate, and that is the number the report prints. On 1,405 trades the bias almost disappears, with an overall loss rate of 68.8% against a shuffled 68.6% in the results below. The bias matters most where people tend to look first, on a new EA with few trades.
The first time I ran the finished script on my demo account, which has only 24 closed trades, it showed the problem clearly. After 3 or more losses in a row came four trades, and three of those lost. That is 75.0%. The overall loss rate was 58% (14 of 24), which already makes 75% look high. The shuffled rate was 45.7%, and 95% of the shuffled rates fell between 0.0% and 85.7%. Against the shuffles p = 0.150. A sample of four trades is far too small to judge anything by.
